 "is_vocab": false,
 "stimulus_html": "<p>“It could neither be surmounted nor doubled, and so they tunneled what looks like a bank-swallow’s hole from a thousand feet below. Powder enough was expended in persuading the iron crags and cliffs to be a thoroughfare to fight half the battles of the Revolution. It was in its time the topmost triumph of engineering nerve and skill in all the world. It stitched the East and the West lovingly together, and who shall say that we are not a United States? . . .</p>\n<p>The blows that sent home the spikes of silver and gold securing the last rail in the laurel were repeated by lightning at Washington and San Francisco, in the length of a heart-beat; blow for blow, from the Potomac to the Pacific. Think of echo answering echo through a sweep of more than three thousand miles! All in all, after the signing of the Declaration of Independence, it was the most impressive and thoughtful ceremony that ever graced the continent. It was electric with the spirit of the New Era.”</p>\n<p align=\"right\"><i>Between the Gates</i>, Benjamin F. Taylor, 1878</p>\n<p><i>Refer to the excerpt provided.</i></p>",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 615676,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "What event does the author describe in this passage?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1170672,
     "content_html": "Inauguration of the Pony Express connecting east and west"
    },
    {
     "id": 1170673,
     "content_html": "Joining of two railroads to complete the first transcontinental railroad"
    },
    {
     "id": 1170671,
     "content_html": "Christening of a riverboat on the Potomac River, celebrated with fireworks"
    },
    {
     "id": 1170670,
     "content_html": "Completion of the Golden Gate Bridge, opening the west to Asian immigrants"
    }
   ],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
